🔹 What Are Default Methods in Interfaces?
A default method is a method with a body in an interface.

Used to provide a default implementation, so implementing classes don't have to override it.

✅ Example:

interface Vehicle {
    default void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle is starting");
    }
}
➤ Usage in a class:

class Car implements Vehicle {
    // inherits start() without overriding
}

✅ Why Use Default Methods?
Backward compatibility — You can add new methods to interfaces without breaking existing code.
// mean if we want default implementation then fine else we override it with new implementation

Enables shared behavior across multiple implementing classes.




🔸 What Are Static Methods in Interfaces?
A static method in an interface belongs to the interface itself, not to instances.

You cannot override it.

Called using the interface name.

✅ Example:

interface Utils {
    static void printMessage() {
        System.out.println("Hello from interface static method");
    }
}
➤ Usage:

Utils.printMessage(); // ✅ Correct

⚠️ Key Differences: default vs static
| Feature           | `default` method      | `static` method                     |
| ----------------- | --------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Can be overridden | ✅ Yes                 | ❌ No                                |
| Called via object | ✅ Yes                 | ❌ No (must be called via interface) |
| Purpose           | Shared implementation | Utility/helper methods              |
| Available since   | Java 8                | Java 8                              |


🚫 Rules & Restrictions
You cannot create abstract + default/static together.

default methods can't be static, and static methods can't be default.

If two interfaces provide the same default method, the class must override it to resolve ambiguity.

🧠 Summary
| Type      | Inherited? | Overridable? | Called via     |
| --------- | ---------- | ------------ | -------------- |
| `default` | ✅ Yes      | ✅ Yes        | Object         |
| `static`  | ❌ No       | ❌ No         | Interface name |
